      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Relief of postoperative pain is a major topic of public health and has been repeatedly shown to be inadequate."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "A questionnaire survey performed in seven European countries (postoperative analgesic therapy observational survey) in 746 health structures--which perform more than 80% of the total number of surgical procedures of each country--has demonstrated significant improvement when compared with previous surveys, particularly regarding increased use of multimodal analgesia and regular administration of analgesic drugs. The present study describes specific results for the 178 French health care institutions surveyed and 345 questionnaires collected. Preoperative patient information is given on a regular basis for 84% of respondents."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Multimodal analgesia is used after major surgery in 87% of cases and prescribed on a regular basis for 84% of respondents. Written protocols are available in 36% of responding institutions. Pain scores are measured several times a day in 65% of institutions. An acute pain service (or any other structure with a similar aim) is found in 52% of institutions. Regular on-site training courses on pain control are delivered to 30% of anaesthesiologists, to 6% of surgeons, to 57% of recovery-room nurses and to 63% of ward nurses. Overall, the study suggests an improvement of current practices but also highlights remaining insufficiencies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Important efforts remain to be done, particularly in the field of initial and continuous education of all healthcare categories as this is believed to be the only real way to obtain long-term results."
        }
      ]},
